# Migration files are checked out with LF on every platform, deliberately.
#
# Prisma records a SHA-256 of each migration.sql in _prisma_migrations and
# compares it on every run. That hash is taken over the file's BYTES, so line
# endings are part of it. With core.autocrlf=true and no rule here, Windows
# checkouts get CRLF while Linux gets LF, and the two platforms therefore
# compute DIFFERENT checksums for a file neither of them edited.
#
# What that looked like in practice: four migrations were recorded with their LF
# hash, Git later rewrote the working copy to CRLF, and Prisma began reporting
# them as modified after they were applied. No SQL had changed - all thirteen
# files matched either the LF or the CRLF form of their stored hash exactly.
# `prisma migrate status` does NOT surface this; it reported "Database schema is
# up to date!" throughout. The damage is that `migrate dev` responds to the
# mismatch by offering to reset the database, and `migrate deploy` refuses to
# run at all.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes the bytes identical on Edward's Windows checkout and in
# Koshi's Linux container, so a migration applied on one machine verifies on the
# other.
*.sql text eol=lf
